And don't forget to keep the back-issues around as an archive! People will 
want to be able to refer back to good articles, and bookmark them. There 
should be the following pages:

blahblah/date/index.html and other html files: contents and articles for 
that date's issue.
blahblah/index.html -- front page logo and contents of current issue, 
linking to articles at blahblah/date/*.html whose URLs won't change when 
the next issue comes out. So people go to an article and bookmark it and 
the bookmark doesn't suddenly point to something else or give 404 errors 
after a few weeks.
The articles should link to their issues' contents page date/index.html so 
that the archived issues remain self-contained.

The "date" directories should use a consistent name format, say yy-mm-dd. 
(With a monthly schedule you might get away with yy-mm only, but if you 
decide to make it more frequent later on you will have a problem with 
having to change the convention for later issues' URLs.)

The front page should link to an index of the back issues, with the 
current issue (date/index.html) linked at the top of the list, and 
progressively older issues' contents pages linked further down.

More generally, check out applicable articles at www.useit.com about Web 
site usability.

Oh, and avoid Javashit, avoid making the content served dependent on how 
you got to it (referrer headers) or other weird and inconsistent behaviors 
that will interfere with bookmarking and violate Web guidelines, and so on 
-- and avoid Flash!

You may take advantage of Web format by having HTML screendumps and 
cmovies -- the latter are large and involve loading a large Java crapplet 
so stick a cmovie on a page of its own with a link to it that lets anyone 
about to click on it know it leads to a page with Java. And avoid PDF...

